      "text": "To solve this problem, we must utilize the properties of ellipses and their relationship with the coordinate axes.\n\nFirstly, we'll consider the coordinates of the tangent points are $(4, 0)$ and $(0, 1)$. This information tells us that the foci of the ellipse must lie on the line passing through these points. Since the foci are equidistant from the endpoint of the major axis (which lies on the line $L$) and the endpoints of the minor axis (which lies on the other line through tangent points), we can easily determine that the midpoint of the line segment connecting the foci must lie at the midpoint of the line segment connecting the tangent points.\n\nThus, the x-coordinates of the foci are $4+4/2=6$ and $0-1/2=-.5$, and the y-coordinates are $1+1/2=1.5$ and $0-4/2=-2$. After we have obtained the foci coordinates, we can determine the distance between the foci of the ellipse using the distance formula:\n$$\\sqrt{(6-0)^2+(0-1.5)^2}=\\boxed{4.33}.$$\n\nThis is the method to solve the problem.",
