      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse, we must first determine the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), as well as the coordinates \\((h, k)\\) of the center of the ellipse. These values are critical in determining the foci of the ellipse.\n\nAn ellipse in the standard form is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} + \\frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((h, k)\\) is the center of the ellipse, \\(a\\) is the semi-major axis, and \\(b\\) is the semi-minor axis. The semi-major axis is the larger of the two, while the semi-minor axis is the smaller.\n\nSince the ellipse is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at \\((4, 0)\\), this tells us that the \\(y\\)-coordinate of the center \\((h, k)\\) is 0, and \\(a = 4\\). Alternatively, because the ellipse is tangent to the \\(y\\)-axis at \\((0, 1)\\), this means the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the center \\((h, k)\\) is 0, and \\(b = 1\\).\n\nSince \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are already known, we can substitute them into the standard form of the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-0)^2}{4^2} + \\frac{(y-0)^2}{1^2} = 1 \\implies \\frac{x^2}{16} + y^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nFrom the equation derived in step one, we can deduce that the distance between the foci of the ellipse is \\(2c\\), where \\(c\\) is calculated as:\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a = 4\\) and \\(b = 1\\), we can substitute and find \\(c\\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{4^2 - 1^2} = \\sqrt{16 - 1} = \\sqrt{15}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{15}}\n\\]",
